dn't speak about the context of all  of this put the full facts into the  public domain while these various  processes are ongoing particularly the  met sure he remains confident that when  everyone when people can see the full  context of what happened it will be  clear that he was straightforward he  said to the house in good faith that he  believed the rules were followed people  might take you at face value but not for  the fact that we have been here before  in precisely the same situation this is  owen patterson volume 2. the  conservative party tries to play games  with your partner with asking me with  parliamentary procedure it's clear that  you don't have the support on your own  back benches to go through with it you  pull it you've got egg in your face it's  another crunching gear change another  embarrassing u-turn well i don't accept  that there's any parallel to the  patterson situation that was a situation  where the standards committee had looked  at something it had come to a conclusion  it had made a recommendation and the  house rebelled  just accepting that the house rebelled  an attempt to change that i think people  concede in retrospect that was a mistake  this is a was around how the the sorry  the privileges committee could look at  all of the events around the events in  downing street and i think the question  the only question was around timing and  as i say the prime minister said on  tuesday it'll be for the house to decide  the prime minister said from india he  has nothing to fear nothing to hide he  looks forward to that process he looks  forward to the full facts and crucially  the context coming out i'm sure he's  looking forward to it i think he's  looking forward to this ending i think  he's looking forward to drawing this to  a conclusion for it to be examined fully  so that we can move on to the things  that people are genuinely concerned  about in terms of cost of living energy  price rises all those other things the  situati